('cp-partition-oct-q05','{"passage":"The permeability of a dissolved compound can depend on more than its affinity for lipid. Researchers compared transport of a monoprotic weak acid, HX, across a protein-free phospholipid barrier. HX has a pKa of 6.0. Its neutral form partitions readily into the barrier, whereas transport of X- through the barrier is negligible. A thin aqueous layer also lies between the stirred bulk solution and each barrier surface.\n\nEqual total concentrations of HX plus X- were placed on the donor side at each pH. The receiver initially contained no compound. Initial transfer rates were measured before donor depletion or receiver accumulation became appreciable. Both compartments were maintained at the indicated pH. Temperature, barrier area, lipid composition, and thickness were identical among trials.\n\nTable 1. Initial transfer rate, in nmol/min. Values are means of four independent barriers; the largest standard deviation was 0.3 nmol/min.\nDonor and receiver pH | Slow stirring | Fast stirring\n5.0 | 4.0 | 8.0\n6.0 | 3.0 | 6.0\n7.0 | 0.9 | 1.0\n\nElectrical measurements and an impermeant reference solute showed comparable barrier integrity at the two stirring rates. A neutral reference compound of similar size showed little pH dependence. Researchers proposed that ionization and transport through the adjacent aqueous layers both contribute to the pattern in Table 1.\n\nIn a separate equilibrium experiment, equal-volume, strongly buffered compartments were maintained at pH 6.0 and pH 7.0. Neutral HX could cross the barrier in either direction, but X- could not. No voltage difference, binding, metabolism, or active transport was present. At equilibrium, the concentration of neutral HX was the same in both compartments. Total concentration was measured after disrupting the barrier.","passage_id":"cp-partition-oct-p01","passage_title":"Ionization and membrane partitioning","passage_order":4,"figures":[]}'::jsonb),
('cp-ultrasound-oct-q06','{"passage":"An imaging group evaluated two ultrasound probes using a tissue-mimicking block containing reflective inclusions. Probe L emitted three-cycle pulses at 2.0 MHz; probe H emitted three-cycle pulses at 4.0 MHz. The speed of sound throughout the block was 1.50 × 10^3 m/s, and each probe both emitted and detected the reflected pulse. The displayed echo time was measured from emission to return at the probe.\n\nTwo thin inclusions lay at depths of 3.00 cm and 3.06 cm along the same beam. For this apparatus, two axial reflectors could be distinguished when their separation was at least one-half of the spatial pulse length. The spatial pulse length equals the number of cycles in a pulse multiplied by its wavelength. Figure 1 shows schematic detected echo envelopes from a separate single-reflector calibration block. Envelope width is schematic and does not depict the duration of the emitted three-cycle pulse. The vertical scale is received peak-voltage amplitude; probe H was calibrated to the same received voltage as L at a shallow reference depth.\n\nAttenuation increased with frequency in this material. The receiver had a fixed noise floor, so a deeper reflector could disappear from the displayed trace without moving. No Doppler processing was used for these stationary inclusions.\n\nThe group also considered a thin matching layer between a probe and the block. Acoustic impedance is Z = ρc, where ρ is density and c is sound speed. For normal incidence at a lossless interface, the fraction of incident intensity reflected is R = [(Z2 - Z1)/(Z2 + Z1)]^2. A test interface had Z1 = 1.0 × 10^6 and Z2 = 3.0 × 10^6 kg m^-2 s^-1. Absorption within this interface was negligible.","passage_id":"cp-ultrasound-oct-p02","passage_title":"Pulse-echo imaging and acoustic interfaces","passage_order":0,"figures":[{"id":"cp-ultrasound-figure1","src":"/figures/pilot-october/ultrasound.png","caption":"Figure 1. Schematic detected echo envelopes from a single-reflector calibration block. The vertical axis gives peak-voltage amplitude, not acoustic intensity. Envelope width is schematic, not the emitted pulse duration.","alt":"Two detected echo envelopes, L and H, share a center at 40 microseconds. L peaks at 0.80 V and H at 0.40 V. Envelope width is schematic.","width":1800,"height":840}]}'::jsonb),
('cp-ultrasound-oct-q07','{"passage":"An imaging group evaluated two ultrasound probes using a tissue-mimicking block containing reflective inclusions. Probe L emitted three-cycle pulses at 2.0 MHz; probe H emitted three-cycle pulses at 4.0 MHz. The speed of sound throughout the block was 1.50 × 10^3 m/s, and each probe both emitted and detected the reflected pulse. The displayed echo time was measured from emission to return at the probe.\n\nTwo thin inclusions lay at depths of 3.00 cm and 3.06 cm along the same beam. For this apparatus, two axial reflectors could be distinguished when their separation was at least one-half of the spatial pulse length. The spatial pulse length equals the number of cycles in a pulse multiplied by its wavelength. Figure 1 shows schematic detected echo envelopes from a separate single-reflector calibration block. Envelope width is schematic and does not depict the duration of the emitted three-cycle pulse. The vertical scale is received peak-voltage amplitude; probe H was calibrated to the same received voltage as L at a shallow reference depth.\n\nAttenuation increased with frequency in this material. The receiver had a fixed noise floor, so a deeper reflector could disappear from the displayed trace without moving. No Doppler processing was used for these stationary inclusions.\n\nThe group also considered a thin matching layer between a probe and the block. Acoustic impedance is Z = ρc, where ρ is density and c is sound speed. For normal incidence at a lossless interface, the fraction of incident intensity reflected is R = [(Z2 - Z1)/(Z2 + Z1)]^2. A test interface had Z1 = 1.0 × 10^6 and Z2 = 3.0 × 10^6 kg m^-2 s^-1. Absorption within this interface was negligible.","passage_id":"cp-ultrasound-oct-p02","passage_title":"Pulse-echo imaging and acoustic interfaces","passage_order":1,"figures":[{"id":"cp-ultrasound-figure1","src":"/figures/pilot-october/ultrasound.png","caption":"Figure 1. Schematic detected echo envelopes from a single-reflector calibration block. The vertical axis gives peak-voltage amplitude, not acoustic intensity. Envelope width is schematic, not the emitted pulse duration.","alt":"Two detected echo envelopes, L and H, share a center at 40 microseconds. L peaks at 0.80 V and H at 0.40 V. Envelope width is schematic.","width":1800,"height":840}]}'::jsonb),
('cp-ultrasound-oct-q08','{"passage":"An imaging group evaluated two ultrasound probes using a tissue-mimicking block containing reflective inclusions. Probe L emitted three-cycle pulses at 2.0 MHz; probe H emitted three-cycle pulses at 4.0 MHz. The speed of sound throughout the block was 1.50 × 10^3 m/s, and each probe both emitted and detected the reflected pulse. The displayed echo time was measured from emission to return at the probe.\n\nTwo thin inclusions lay at depths of 3.00 cm and 3.06 cm along the same beam. For this apparatus, two axial reflectors could be distinguished when their separation was at least one-half of the spatial pulse length. The spatial pulse length equals the number of cycles in a pulse multiplied by its wavelength. Figure 1 shows schematic detected echo envelopes from a separate single-reflector calibration block. Envelope width is schematic and does not depict the duration of the emitted three-cycle pulse. The vertical scale is received peak-voltage amplitude; probe H was calibrated to the same received voltage as L at a shallow reference depth.\n\nAttenuation increased with frequency in this material. The receiver had a fixed noise floor, so a deeper reflector could disappear from the displayed trace without moving. No Doppler processing was used for these stationary inclusions.\n\nThe group also considered a thin matching layer between a probe and the block. Acoustic impedance is Z = ρc, where ρ is density and c is sound speed. For normal incidence at a lossless interface, the fraction of incident intensity reflected is R = [(Z2 - Z1)/(Z2 + Z1)]^2. A test interface had Z1 = 1.0 × 10^6 and Z2 = 3.0 × 10^6 kg m^-2 s^-1. Absorption within this interface was negligible.","passage_id":"cp-ultrasound-oct-p02","passage_title":"Pulse-echo imaging and acoustic interfaces","passage_order":2,"figures":[{"id":"cp-ultrasound-figure1","src":"/figures/pilot-october/ultrasound.png","caption":"Figure 1. Schematic detected echo envelopes from a single-reflector calibration block. The vertical axis gives peak-voltage amplitude, not acoustic intensity. Envelope width is schematic, not the emitted pulse duration.","alt":"Two detected echo envelopes, L and H, share a center at 40 microseconds. L peaks at 0.80 V and H at 0.40 V. Envelope width is schematic.","width":1800,"height":840}]}'::jsonb),
('cp-ultrasound-oct-q09','{"passage":"An imaging group evaluated two ultrasound probes using a tissue-mimicking block containing reflective inclusions. Probe L emitted three-cycle pulses at 2.0 MHz; probe H emitted three-cycle pulses at 4.0 MHz. The speed of sound throughout the block was 1.50 × 10^3 m/s, and each probe both emitted and detected the reflected pulse. The displayed echo time was measured from emission to return at the probe.\n\nTwo thin inclusions lay at depths of 3.00 cm and 3.06 cm along the same beam. For this apparatus, two axial reflectors could be distinguished when their separation was at least one-half of the spatial pulse length. The spatial pulse length equals the number of cycles in a pulse multiplied by its wavelength. Figure 1 shows schematic detected echo envelopes from a separate single-reflector calibration block. Envelope width is schematic and does not depict the duration of the emitted three-cycle pulse. The vertical scale is received peak-voltage amplitude; probe H was calibrated to the same received voltage as L at a shallow reference depth.\n\nAttenuation increased with frequency in this material. The receiver had a fixed noise floor, so a deeper reflector could disappear from the displayed trace without moving. No Doppler processing was used for these stationary inclusions.\n\nThe group also considered a thin matching layer between a probe and the block. Acoustic impedance is Z = ρc, where ρ is density and c is sound speed. For normal incidence at a lossless interface, the fraction of incident intensity reflected is R = [(Z2 - Z1)/(Z2 + Z1)]^2. A test interface had Z1 = 1.0 × 10^6 and Z2 = 3.0 × 10^6 kg m^-2 s^-1. Absorption within this interface was negligible.","passage_id":"cp-ultrasound-oct-p02","passage_title":"Pulse-echo imaging and acoustic interfaces","passage_order":3,"figures":[{"id":"cp-ultrasound-figure1","src":"/figures/pilot-october/ultrasound.png","caption":"Figure 1. Schematic detected echo envelopes from a single-reflector calibration block. The vertical axis gives peak-voltage amplitude, not acoustic intensity. Envelope width is schematic, not the emitted pulse duration.","alt":"Two detected echo envelopes, L and H, share a center at 40 microseconds. L peaks at 0.80 V and H at 0.40 V. Envelope width is schematic.","width":1800,"height":840}]}'::jsonb),
('cp-ultrasound-oct-q10','{"passage":"An imaging group evaluated two ultrasound probes using a tissue-mimicking block containing reflective inclusions. Probe L emitted three-cycle pulses at 2.0 MHz; probe H emitted three-cycle pulses at 4.0 MHz. The speed of sound throughout the block was 1.50 × 10^3 m/s, and each probe both emitted and detected the reflected pulse. The displayed echo time was measured from emission to return at the probe.\n\nTwo thin inclusions lay at depths of 3.00 cm and 3.06 cm along the same beam. For this apparatus, two axial reflectors could be distinguished when their separation was at least one-half of the spatial pulse length. The spatial pulse length equals the number of cycles in a pulse multiplied by its wavelength. Figure 1 shows schematic detected echo envelopes from a separate single-reflector calibration block. Envelope width is schematic and does not depict the duration of the emitted three-cycle pulse. The vertical scale is received peak-voltage amplitude; probe H was calibrated to the same received voltage as L at a shallow reference depth.\n\nAttenuation increased with frequency in this material. The receiver had a fixed noise floor, so a deeper reflector could disappear from the displayed trace without moving. No Doppler processing was used for these stationary inclusions.\n\nThe group also considered a thin matching layer between a probe and the block. Acoustic impedance is Z = ρc, where ρ is density and c is sound speed. For normal incidence at a lossless interface, the fraction of incident intensity reflected is R = [(Z2 - Z1)/(Z2 + Z1)]^2. A test interface had Z1 = 1.0 × 10^6 and Z2 = 3.0 × 10^6 kg m^-2 s^-1. Absorption within this interface was negligible.","passage_id":"cp-ultrasound-oct-p02","passage_title":"Pulse-echo imaging and acoustic interfaces","passage_order":4,"figures":[{"id":"cp-ultrasound-figure1","src":"/figures/pilot-october/ultrasound.png","caption":"Figure 1. Schematic detected echo envelopes from a single-reflector calibration block. The vertical axis gives peak-voltage amplitude, not acoustic intensity. Envelope width is schematic, not the emitted pulse duration.","alt":"Two detected echo envelopes, L and H, share a center at 40 microseconds. L peaks at 0.80 V and H at 0.40 V. Envelope width is schematic.","width":1800,"height":840}]}'::jsonb),
('bb-renal-oct-q14','{"passage":"Researchers investigated renal handling of three freely filtered solutes before and after exposure to Compound T. Solute I was neither secreted nor reabsorbed. Solute S could undergo tubular secretion and had negligible reabsorption. Solute Y could be reabsorbed but was not secreted. None of the solutes was produced or degraded in the kidney, and arterial concentrations were stable during each collection. Renal clearance denotes the volume of arterial plasma containing the amount of solute excreted per minute.\n\nTable 2. Steady-state concentrations and urine collection measurements. Arterial and urinary concentrations are in mg/mL; urine flow is in mL/min. Values represent averages from six paired observations.\nCondition | Urine flow | Arterial I | Urinary I | Arterial S | Urinary S | Arterial Y | Urinary Y\nBefore T | 1.0 | 0.10 | 10.0 | 0.020 | 10.0 | 1.0 | 20.0\nAfter T | 2.0 | 0.10 | 5.0 | 0.020 | 3.0 | 1.0 | 10.0\n\nRenal venous S concentrations were 0.0020 mg/mL before T and 0.0092 mg/mL after T. The extraction fraction for S is the fraction of its arterial concentration removed in a single passage through the kidney: (arterial concentration - renal venous concentration)/arterial concentration. At steady state, the amount extracted from incoming plasma per minute equals the amount excreted in urine per minute. Urine flow was negligible compared with renal plasma flow for this calculation.\n\nSeparate measurements found no appreciable change in hematocrit or systemic arterial pressure. Urine osmolality fell from 600 to 300 mOsm/kg after T. Plasma osmolality was unchanged. The investigators considered both altered vascular delivery and altered tubular processing as explanations for the observations.","passage_id":"bb-renal-oct-p03","passage_title":"Renal handling and extraction","passage_order":0,"figures":[]}'::jsonb),
