('bb-renal-oct-q17','{"passage":"Researchers investigated renal handling of three freely filtered solutes before and after exposure to Compound T. Solute I was neither secreted nor reabsorbed. Solute S could undergo tubular secretion and had negligible reabsorption. Solute Y could be reabsorbed but was not secreted. None of the solutes was produced or degraded in the kidney, and arterial concentrations were stable during each collection. Renal clearance denotes the volume of arterial plasma containing the amount of solute excreted per minute.\n\nTable 2. Steady-state concentrations and urine collection measurements. Arterial and urinary concentrations are in mg/mL; urine flow is in mL/min. Values represent averages from six paired observations.\nCondition | Urine flow | Arterial I | Urinary I | Arterial S | Urinary S | Arterial Y | Urinary Y\nBefore T | 1.0 | 0.10 | 10.0 | 0.020 | 10.0 | 1.0 | 20.0\nAfter T | 2.0 | 0.10 | 5.0 | 0.020 | 3.0 | 1.0 | 10.0\n\nRenal venous S concentrations were 0.0020 mg/mL before T and 0.0092 mg/mL after T. The extraction fraction for S is the fraction of its arterial concentration removed in a single passage through the kidney: (arterial concentration - renal venous concentration)/arterial concentration. At steady state, the amount extracted from incoming plasma per minute equals the amount excreted in urine per minute. Urine flow was negligible compared with renal plasma flow for this calculation.\n\nSeparate measurements found no appreciable change in hematocrit or systemic arterial pressure. Urine osmolality fell from 600 to 300 mOsm/kg after T. Plasma osmolality was unchanged. The investigators considered both altered vascular delivery and altered tubular processing as explanations for the observations.","passage_id":"bb-renal-oct-p03","passage_title":"Renal handling and extraction","passage_order":3,"figures":[]}'::jsonb),
