('bb-coupling-oct-q19','{"passage":"Electron transport in mitochondria couples oxidation of respiratory substrates to a proton electrochemical gradient across the inner membrane. Protons can return to the matrix through ATP synthase or through other routes. Researchers compared equal amounts of mitochondrial protein from a reference preparation and a preparation exposed to Compound Q. Both received the same substrate mixture, ADP, and phosphate under conditions in which oxygen and substrate availability did not limit the measured rates.\n\nFor each preparation, oxygen consumption was measured at baseline, after addition of an ATP synthase blocker B, after addition of a proton carrier U in the continued presence of B, and finally after addition of an electron-transport blocker E. U permits proton movement through the inner membrane independently of ATP synthase. B and E were shown to retain their target effects in the Q-exposed preparation. Figure 2A shows the stabilized rate in each phase. A separate matched experiment found that U alone reached the same rates as B followed by U.\n\nFigure 2B shows baseline ATP synthesis and the matrix-to-intermembrane-space pH difference measured in separate samples. Higher positive values indicate that the matrix is more alkaline. ATP measurements excluded ATP formed outside mitochondria. Matrix substrate concentrations and the abundance of the measured respiratory complexes were similar in the two preparations. An unrelated matrix enzyme retained comparable activity.\n\nThe investigators used the decline in respiration after B as an operational estimate of the ATP-synthesis-linked component of baseline respiration. Oxygen consumption remaining after E was treated as background. They also examined whether Q changed the relationship between electron transfer and ATP production rather than simply lowering the abundance of respiratory proteins.","passage_id":"bb-coupling-oct-p04","passage_title":"Respiratory coupling and assay interpretation","passage_order":0,"figures":[{"id":"bb-coupling-figure2","src":"/figures/pilot-october/coupling.png","caption":"Figure 2. A: Oxygen consumption during the sequential phases. B1: Baseline mitochondrial ATP synthesis. B2: Matrix-minus-intermembrane-space pH difference. Bars show means of six preparations; error bars are SD. Oxygen and ATP rates are normalized to matched amounts of mitochondrial protein.","alt":"Oxygen consumption in pmol O2/min: reference versus Q-exposed, baseline 100 versus 100; after B, 35 versus 65; after B plus U, 180 versus 180; after B plus U plus E, 10 versus 10. Baseline ATP synthesis in pmol/min: 180 versus 90. Matrix-minus-intermembrane pH difference: 1.0 versus 0.5. Each mean uses six preparations. SD is 4 for every oxygen bar, 8 for every ATP bar, and 0.05 for each pH bar.","width":2100,"height":850}]}'::jsonb),
