('ps-detection-oct-q27','{"passage":"A research team studied how incentives and competing tasks affect reports of a faint visual target. In every block, a target was present on 400 trials and absent on 400 trials. The target''s contrast, the background, and the proportion of target-present trials were identical across conditions. Participants pressed one key for \"present\" and another for \"absent.\" A hit was a \"present\" response on a target-present trial; a false alarm was a \"present\" response on a target-absent trial.\n\nEach participant completed three conditions in counterbalanced order. In the standard condition, correct detections and correct rejections received equal rewards. In the missed-target penalty condition, missing a target produced a larger monetary loss than falsely reporting a target. In the divided-attention condition, standard rewards applied, but participants also classified a concurrent tone as high or low. Tone accuracy averaged 84%. Participants practiced the response keys before all conditions.\n\nFigure 3 summarizes visual responses. To help separate sensitivity from response criterion, the researchers used an equal-variance signal-detection model. Sensitivity was d'' = z(hit rate) - z(false-alarm rate), where z gives the corresponding position on a standard normal distribution. The approximate values needed for these data are listed below.\n\nTable 3. Normal-distribution transformations.\nRate | 0.16 | 0.31 | 0.62 | 0.69 | 0.84\nz(rate) | -1.0 | -0.5 | 0.3 | 0.5 | 1.0\n\nA follow-up training condition sounded an unpleasant tone after a missed visual target. The tone immediately stopped when the participant pressed a designated correction key. Over training, correction-key responses became more frequent. This tone was distinct from the high/low classification task, and the correction response did not change whether the visual target had originally been detected.","passage_id":"ps-detection-oct-p05","passage_title":"Detection, response criterion, and divided attention","passage_order":0,"figures":[{"id":"ps-detection-figure3","src":"/figures/pilot-october/detection.png","caption":"Figure 3. Mean visual-response counts per 400 target-present and 400 target-absent trials. Counts are rounded aggregate means from 40 participants.","alt":"Mean response counts: standard 276 hits and 64 false alarms; missed-target penalty 336 hits and 124 false alarms; divided attention 248 hits and 124 false alarms. Each condition contains 400 target-present and 400 target-absent trials. Means are from 40 participants.","width":1800,"height":840}]}'::jsonb),
