('ps-neighborhood-oct-q32','{"passage":"A city renovated parks in neighborhoods selected through an infrastructure-priority program. Researchers compared residents near six renovated parks with residents near six eligible parks scheduled for later renovation. Neighborhoods were matched on baseline median income, population density, and access to public transit. Researchers did not assign renovation timing. The same adult residents were invited to complete a survey and wear an activity monitor before renovation and one year later. Measurements were collected in the same season at both time points.\n\nTable 4. Mean activity and social measures among residents who completed both assessments. Each group included 180 residents.\nMeasure | Renovation before | Renovation after | Comparison before | Comparison after\nModerate-to-vigorous activity, min/week | 90 | 120 | 100 | 110\nPerceived neighborhood trust, 1-5 scale | 2.8 | 3.4 | 2.9 | 3.0\nOrganized-group participation, percent | 20 | 35 | 22 | 24\n\nInterviews suggested several possible routes from renovation to behavior. Some residents described watching neighbors use new exercise equipment and later trying the same routines. Others reported that walking groups made them feel capable of being active despite earlier unsuccessful attempts. A third group emphasized safer paths and lighting rather than interactions with other residents.\n\nAt baseline, 240 residents in each group enrolled. Residents lost to follow-up had lower baseline activity on average than those retained. A separate survey found that household turnover was higher in renovation neighborhoods. The researchers distinguished an individual resident''s perceived ability to exercise from shared expectations that neighbors would cooperate to maintain public spaces. They also distinguished relationships within existing groups from connections between groups that rarely interacted before renovation.\n\nThe project examined whether changes in physical environments and social relationships might jointly influence activity. Matching was intended to improve comparability, but renovation timing remained part of a municipal process rather than a randomized experiment.","passage_id":"ps-neighborhood-oct-p06","passage_title":"Built environment, social ties, and causal inference","passage_order":0,"figures":[]}'::jsonb),
