ts and social relationships might jointly influence activity. Matching was intended to improve comparability, but renovation timing remained part of a municipal process rather than a randomized experiment.","passage_id":"ps-neighborhood-oct-p06","passage_title":"Built environment, social ties, and causal inference","passage_order":1,"figures":[]}'::jsonb),
('ps-neighborhood-oct-q34','{"passage":"A city renovated parks in neighborhoods selected through an infrastructure-priority program. Researchers compared residents near six renovated parks with residents near six eligible parks scheduled for later renovation. Neighborhoods were matched on baseline median income, population density, and access to public transit. Researchers did not assign renovation timing. The same adult residents were invited to complete a survey and wear an activity monitor before renovation and one year later. Measurements were collected in the same season at both time points.\n\nTable 4. Mean activity and social measures among residents who completed both assessments. Each group included 180 residents.\nMeasure | Renovation before | Renovation after | Comparison before | Comparison after\nModerate-to-vigorous activity, min/week | 90 | 120 | 100 | 110\nPerceived neighborhood trust, 1-5 scale | 2.8 | 3.4 | 2.9 | 3.0\nOrganized-group participation, percent | 20 | 35 | 22 | 24\n\nInterviews suggested several possible routes from renovation to behavior. Some residents described watching neighbors use new exercise equipment and later trying the same routines. Others reported that walking groups made them feel capable of being active despite earlier unsuccessful attempts. A third group emphasized safer paths and lighting rather than interactions with other residents.\n\nAt baseline, 240 residents in each group enrolled. Residents lost to follow-up had lower baseline activity on average than those retained. A separate survey found that household turnover was higher in renovation neighborhoods. The researchers distinguished an individual resident''s perceived ability to exercise from shared expectations that neighbors would cooperate to maintain public spaces. They also distinguished relationships within existing groups from connections between groups that rarely interacted before renovation.\n\nThe project examined whether changes in physical environments and social relationships might jointly influence activity. Matching was intended to improve comparability, but renovation timing remained part of a municipal process rather than a randomized experiment.","passage_id":"ps-neighborhood-oct-p06","passage_title":"Built environment, social ties, and causal inference","passage_order":2,"figures":[]}'::jsonb),
('ps-neighborhood-oct-q35','{"passage":"A city renovated parks in neighborhoods selected through an infrastructure-priority program. Researchers compared residents near six renovated parks with residents near six eligible parks scheduled for later renovation. Neighborhoods were matched on baseline median income, population density, and access to public transit. Researchers did not assign renovation timing. The same adult residents were invited to complete a survey and wear an activity monitor before renovation and one year later. Measurements were collected in the same season at both time points.\n\nTable 4. Mean activity and social measures among residents who completed both assessments. Each group included 180 residents.\nMeasure | Renovation before | Renovation after | Comparison before | Comparison after\nModerate-to-vigorous activity, min/week | 90 | 120 | 100 | 110\nPerceived neighborhood trust, 1-5 scale | 2.8 | 3.4 | 2.9 | 3.0\nOrganized-group participation, percent | 20 | 35 | 22 | 24\n\nInterviews suggested several possible routes from renovation to behavior. Some residents described watching neighbors use new exercise equipment and later trying the same routines. Others reported that walking groups made them feel capable of being active despite earlier unsuccessful attempts. A third group emphasized safer paths and lighting rather than interactions with other residents.\n\nAt baseline, 240 residents in each group enrolled. Residents lost to follow-up had lower baseline activity on average than those retained. A separate survey found that household turnover was higher in renovation neighborhoods. The researchers distinguished an individual resident''s perceived ability to exercise from shared expectations that neighbors would cooperate to maintain public spaces. They also distinguished relationships within existing groups from connections between groups that rarely interacted before renovation.\n\nThe project examined whether changes in physical environments and social relationships might jointly influence activity. Matching was intended to improve comparability, but renovation timing remained part of a municipal process rather than a randomized experiment.","passage_id":"ps-neighborhood-oct-p06","passage_title":"Built environment, social ties, and causal inference","passage_order":3,"figures":[]}'::jsonb),
('ps-neighborhood-oct-q36','{"passage":"A city renovated parks in neighborhoods selected through an infrastructure-priority program. Researchers compared residents near six renovated parks with residents near six eligible parks scheduled for later renovation. Neighborhoods were matched on baseline median income, population density, and access to public transit. Researchers did not assign renovation timing. The same adult residents were invited to complete a survey and wear an activity monitor before renovation and one year later. Measurements were collected in the same season at both time points.\n\nTable 4. Mean activity and social measures among residents who completed both assessments. Each group included 180 residents.\nMeasure | Renovation before | Renovation after | Comparison before | Comparison after\nModerate-to-vigorous activity, min/week | 90 | 120 | 100 | 110\nPerceived neighborhood trust, 1-5 scale | 2.8 | 3.4 | 2.9 | 3.0\nOrganized-group participation, percent | 20 | 35 | 22 | 24\n\nInterviews suggested several possible routes from renovation to behavior. Some residents described watching neighbors use new exercise equipment and later trying the same routines. Others reported that walking groups made them feel capable of being active despite earlier unsuccessful attempts. A third group emphasized safer paths and lighting rather than interactions with other residents.\n\nAt baseline, 240 residents in each group enrolled. Residents lost to follow-up had lower baseline activity on average than those retained. A separate survey found that household turnover was higher in renovation neighborhoods. The researchers distinguished an individual resident''s perceived ability to exercise from shared expectations that neighbors would cooperate to maintain public spaces. They also distinguished relationships within existing groups from connections between groups that rarely interacted before renovation.\n\nThe project examined whether changes in physical environments and social relationships might jointly influence activity. Matching was intended to improve comparability, but renovation timing remained part of a municipal process rather than a randomized experiment.","passage_id":"ps-neighborhood-oct-p06","passage_title":"Built environment, social ties, and causal inference","passage_order":4,"figures":[]}'::jsonb),
('cars-repair-oct-q39','{"passage":"The old station acquired its celebrated roof almost by accident. The architect''s drawing specified tiles; a shortage supplied corrugated metal instead. When a preservation committee proposed replacing the metal with tiles, it called the project a return to authenticity. The roof that had actually sheltered generations of passengers was thus condemned for failing to resemble a roof that had never existed. The episode is amusing only until one notices how often a building''s surviving life must defend itself against its paperwork.\n\nArchitectural drawings deserve attention. They record ambitions that an unfinished or altered building may obscure. But intention is not the sole source of historical authority. A station is also a succession of bargains: between designer and builder, weather and material, occupants and the schedules that bring them together. To freeze one moment as the original is to select from that succession, not to escape selection. A committee that chooses the architect''s drawing has made an interpretation just as surely as one that chooses the familiar metal roof.\n\nThe most persuasive defense of preservation is therefore less theatrical than a recovery of origins. Old buildings make earlier decisions available for inspection. Their unevenness can expose how a city accommodated uses that no planner anticipated. This evidentiary value is easily destroyed, but it is not secured merely by keeping a facade. A perfectly retained street elevation may conceal an interior from which every trace of working life has been erased. What survives for a photograph need not survive for inquiry.\n\nThat distinction should trouble admirers of adaptive reuse as well as its opponents. A warehouse converted into apartments can retain the name of its former industry while removing the loading doors, circulation routes, and divisions that made the industry intelligible. The conversion may be worthwhile; new housing has claims of its own. Calling it preservation, however, should not settle the question of what evidence it preserves. The language of rescue can allow a developer to present a choice among public goods as though no choice had occurred.\n\nConversely, an insistence on keeping every worn stair or obsolete pipe can mistake accumulated matter for accumulated knowledge. Some replacements make a building usable enough to remain part of public life. Maintenance is not an interruption of history; it is one of the activities history consists of. Yet this observation grants no blanket permission to modernize. The relevant question is whether a particular alteration sustains the building''s intelligibility or suppresses it, and to whom. An engineer, a former worker, and a hurried commuter may read different evidence in the same space.\n\nA preservation report ought, then, to describe its losses as candidly as its achievements. It should distinguish what is documented, what is inferred, and what has been deliberately newly made. Such a report would give up the comforting claim that the building has simply been returned to itself. In exchange, it would offer something more durable: a record that later users can dispute. The honest building need not announce every intervention on its walls. The honest institution must nevertheless leave the argument open, rather than hide its own decisions behind the supposed command of the past.","passage_id":"cars-repair-oct-p07","passage_title":"Architecture: preservation and the authority of repair","passage_order":0,"figures":[]}'::jsonb),
