('cars-measures-oct-q45','{"passage":"At a meeting about library funding, the most confident speaker was a spreadsheet. Its columns listed visits, loans, and cost per transaction; branches arranged themselves into an apparently natural ranking. Nobody had to say that borrowing a novel was the same kind of achievement as using a quiet table to fill out a housing application. The table had performed that assimilation already, counting the former and leaving the latter difficult to see. Numbers had not ended the argument about a library''s purpose. They had advanced one side of it without acquiring a speaker''s name.\n\nThis is not an objection to counting. A librarian who reports an increase in evening attendance may reveal a need that anecdotes missed. A common measure can also restrain favoritism: it is harder to reward a politically connected branch for its vague reputation when another can demonstrate extensive use. There is real democratic value in asking institutions to show what they do. The trouble begins when showing becomes synonymous with whatever a reporting system happens to record.\n\nMeasures are often defended as imperfect but better than nothing. The defense conceals a choice. A figure may be compared with a different figure, with a narrative account, or with a combination of observations. \"Nothing\" is frequently the name given to evidence that an administrative system has not learned to process. The person explaining that a library is the neighborhood''s only heated public room has supplied information, even if no column awaits it. Converting that fact into a number could make it travel farther through the budget process; it could also detach it from the circumstances that explain its importance.\n\nThe demand that every benefit receive a common unit promises comparability at a price. It can make unlike services appear interchangeable and turn a decision about purposes into a calculation about efficiency. Yet refusing comparison altogether has its own price. Funds are finite, and a claim that one institution is incomparable may protect it from scrutiny rather than illuminate its value. A careful public argument must therefore compare while preserving an account of what the comparison leaves out. The spreadsheet should be an exhibit in that argument, not its constitution.\n\nThere is a further complication. Once a measure distributes rewards, institutions adapt to it. A branch judged by loans may encourage short, frequent borrowing or avoid services that take time without producing a transaction. The resulting improvement in the reported figure is not necessarily fictitious: more books may indeed circulate. But the figure becomes a record of both the original activity and the institution''s response to being measured. Treating it as an untouched window on performance ignores a change produced by the window itself.\n\nThe practical remedy is to separate routine records from high-stakes rewards. Institutions can still report counts, but funding reviews should periodically examine a sample of actual encounters, using questions chosen to test the institution''s stated purposes rather than merely repeat its target. Such reviews cost time and can be conducted badly. Their purpose is not to find an unmeasured truth; it is to create a second view whose errors need not follow the incentives embedded in the first. If both views agree, the numerical claim gains support. If they diverge, the discrepancy is information, not administrative failure.","passage_id":"cars-measures-oct-p08","passage_title":"Public policy: the work done by measures","passage_order":0,"figures":[]}'::jsonb),
