own price. Funds are finite, and a claim that one institution is incomparable may protect it from scrutiny rather than illuminate its value. A careful public argument must therefore compare while preserving an account of what the comparison leaves out. The spreadsheet should be an exhibit in that argument, not its constitution.\n\nThere is a further complication. Once a measure distributes rewards, institutions adapt to it. A branch judged by loans may encourage short, frequent borrowing or avoid services that take time without producing a transaction. The resulting improvement in the reported figure is not necessarily fictitious: more books may indeed circulate. But the figure becomes a record of both the original activity and the institution''s response to being measured. Treating it as an untouched window on performance ignores a change produced by the window itself.\n\nThe practical remedy is to separate routine records from high-stakes rewards. Institutions can still report counts, but funding reviews should periodically examine a sample of actual encounters, using questions chosen to test the institution''s stated purposes rather than merely repeat its target. Such reviews cost time and can be conducted badly. Their purpose is not to find an unmeasured truth; it is to create a second view whose errors need not follow the incentives embedded in the first. If both views agree, the numerical claim gains support. If they diverge, the discrepancy is information, not administrative failure.","passage_id":"cars-measures-oct-p08","passage_title":"Public policy: the work done by measures","passage_order":3,"figures":[]}'::jsonb),
